Stop iconv looping when no character fits in a full input buffer.
process_file() treats EINVAL from iconv() as an incomplete character at
the end of the buffer: it keeps the unconverted bytes, reads more and
tries again, failing only at end of file. When the unconverted bytes
fill the whole buffer, fread() is asked for zero bytes, so end of file
is never reached and the same buffer is retried forever. This happens
with a multibyte code page whose length function always reports EINVAL,
as do the code pages with characters longer than two bytes that have no
converter of their own, such as UTF-7 (65000): iconv -f 65000 hung on
input of 512 bytes or more, while shorter input failed with "Invalid
argument". Fail as at end of file when EINVAL comes with a full buffer,
since no character can be that long.

---
 miscutils/iconv.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/miscutils/iconv.c
+++ b/miscutils/iconv.c
@@ -1691,7 +1691,8 @@
 		r = iconv(cd, &pin, &inbytesleft, &pout, &outbytesleft);
 		fwrite(outbuf, 1, sizeof(outbuf) - outbytesleft, out);
 		if (r == (size_t)(-1) && errno != E2BIG &&
-				(errno != EINVAL || feof(in)))
+				(errno != EINVAL || feof(in) ||
+				inbytesleft == sizeof(inbuf)))
 			bb_perror_msg_and_die("conversion error");
 		memmove(inbuf, pin, inbytesleft);
 		rest = inbytesleft;
